> modern, threaded news reader, when such are
> available "for free" and essentially everywhere.

A threaded news reader doesn't solve the problem, nor does Google.
It's entirely possible for your reply to arrive at my news server
before the message you're replying to.  It's entirely possible that the
message you're replying to has been expired already and is not
available on my news server.  It's entirely possible that the message
you're replying to has X-No-Archive set and is not archived on Google.
And it's entirely possible that Google's broken attempt at threading
doesn't actually capture the proper parent post, making the context
very difficult to find.

In short, the _design_ of Usenet is that you cannot be certain that any
other post is visible at someone's server at the same time yours is.
Quoting context is not catering to some buggy newsreader or some
luddite antiquated notion, it's simply having a basic awareness of the
fundamental (intentional) design of Usenet.

It also makes your post readable if someone searches the archives and
finds it, but the parent post was not archived.



